content.theory:
Pseudo-classes select an element in a **particular state** — the single colon targets conditions the HTML alone cannot express. The most famous is `:hover`, which applies while the pointer is over an element:

```css
a:hover { color: #2563eb; }
```
Pseudo-classes let you build interactivity and pattern styling without adding classes to the markup.

### :focus & :focus-visible
`:focus` matches any focused element, whether it received focus from a mouse click, a Tab press, or a script. Keyboard users *need* a visible focus indicator, but many designers find a focus ring ugly after a mouse click — that's exactly what `:focus-visible` is for: it only matches when focus came from the **keyboard or an assistive device**. Use `:focus-visible` for subtle outlines, but always keep some visible focus indication for accessibility.

### Structural pseudo-classes
```css
li:first-child { font-weight: 700; }
li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
li:nth-child(2) { background: #f3f4f6; }
li:nth-child(odd) { background: #eef2ff; }
```
`:nth-child()` accepts formulas like `2n+1` plus the keywords `odd` and `even` — perfect for zebra-striping tables and lists. Note that `:nth-child` counts *all* siblings; `:nth-of-type` counts only siblings with the same tag.

### :not()
```css
p:not(.intro) { color: #444; }
```
`:not()` inverts a selector — it matches everything that is *not* the argument. Modern browsers support selector lists, so `:not(.a, .b)` works too, letting you exclude several cases in one rule.

### Pseudo-elements ::before and ::after
Pseudo-**classes** select states; pseudo-**elements** (double colon) generate fake children. `::before` inserts content at the very start of an element, `::after` at the very end — and both **require the `content` property**, otherwise nothing renders:

```css
li::before {
  content: '> ';
  color: #6d28d9;
}
```
Pseudo-elements are ideal for decorative icons, arrows, quotes, and labels — visual detail with zero extra HTML.

### Attribute selectors
```css
input[type='email'] { border-color: #10b981; }
a[href^='https']::after { content: ' 🔗'; }
```
`[attr=value]` matches exactly, `[attr^=]` starts-with, `[attr$=]` ends-with, and `[attr*=]` contains — handy for styling input variants and flagging external links.

The common mistakes: adding `:hover` styling but forgetting the focused state for keyboard users, and writing a `::before`/`::after` without `content` (it then silently never renders).
